Found it. When `redis.incr()` encounters an error, it returns `null` instead of throwing (line 22 in redis-client.js). Back in limiter.js, the check is:

```javascript
const count = await redis.incr(key);
// ...
if (count > MAX_REQUESTS) {
  return res.status(429).json({ error: 'rate limit exceeded' });
}
return next();
```

When `count` is `null`:
- `null > 100` evaluates to `false`
- The 429 check is skipped
- The request is allowed through

So if Redis was down or experiencing errors yesterday, every request that hit `incr()` would get through unthrottled. A Redis blip or timeout would disable the entire rate limiter for that minute. The error-swallowing pattern in redis-client.js (meant to prevent 500s) instead silently breaks rate limiting.

